00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:21.780
Let's talk about fertility.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.120
And in doing so, let's take
a step back from this word

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.450
"fertility" and ask, what is
fertility and fertilization

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.690
really all about?

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.770
Well, the obvious answer is that
it's about producing offspring.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.950
But more importantly, it's
about producing offspring

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.610
that contain the genetic
components of both parents

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:41.820
and indeed contain half of
the genes from one parent

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.740
and half of the genes
from another parent.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.730
Now, there are two general
types of cells in the body.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:48.480
The most common types
of cells in the body

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.570
are called somatic cells.

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:51.403
So these would be all
the cells in your body

00:08:51.403 --> 00:08:54.900
except the egg in females
and the sperm in males.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.330
The egg in females
and the sperm males

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.040
are part of what's
called the germline.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:00.998
And again, it has nothing
to do with infection.

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:05.340
It's just that the cells of the
germline have genes that cannot

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.300
be modified by the behavior
of the individual that houses

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.200
those genes.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.260
What do I mean by that?

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:14.310
Well, if I were to tell you that
by exercising you can improve

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:18.540
mitochondrial function,
you can change hormones

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:20.910
by reducing stress,
you can reduce cortisol

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:24.360
by hitting puberty,
for instance, you

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.845
will have the secretion of
hormones that then change

00:09:26.845 --> 00:09:28.470
gene expression in
other cells, leading

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:31.140
to the development of
body hair, facial hair,

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.925
deepening the voice,
breast growth, et cetera,

00:09:33.925 --> 00:09:34.800
you'd say, OK, great.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.910
Yeah, that all
makes perfect sense.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:39.600
But that's all occurring in
the so-called somatic cells.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:41.910
The germ cells or
the germline cells--

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.980
that is, the egg and the sperm--

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.260
are a very unique
and protected set

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:49.470
of cells that are generated
in a particular way

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:55.272
and whose genetic components are
not modifiable by experience.

00:09:55.272 --> 00:09:57.480
And when you take a step
back and you think about it,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.563
you say, oh, that's right.

00:09:58.563 --> 00:10:00.990
There's no reason to
think that exercising

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:05.020
will make the children that
you have not yet had stronger.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:06.270
And you say, of course not.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:07.090
Well, why is that?

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:09.270
Well, that's because
there is a barrier

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.010
between the genes of the
germline cells and behaviors.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.620
They cannot be modified by
behaviors and the various

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.390
things that you do
in your lifetime.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:19.920
Now, I suppose
there's an exception

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.960
in the negative direction.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:24.300
And what I'm referring to
here is if you were to, say,

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.150
be exposed to a chemical
that could mutate the DNA

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:32.010
of your egg or sperm or if you
were to fertilize an embryo

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.070
in a certain way or at a certain
stage of life that it got

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:36.528
an extra chromosome,
for instance--

00:10:36.528 --> 00:10:38.278
we'll talk about this
a little bit later--

00:10:38.278 --> 00:10:39.960
well, then, of course,
you could end up

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.240
with offspring
that have modified

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.750
DNA that don't faithfully
represent half of the genes

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.210
from mom and half of
the genes from dad.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.390
But that's not the same
as specific behaviors

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:53.550
modifying the genes
of those cells--

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.320
the sperm and the egg cells--

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:58.710
in a way that improves
the offspring.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.613
So the key first thing
to understand today

00:11:00.613 --> 00:11:03.030
is that there's a distinction
between somatic cells, which

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:04.440
is the vast majority
of cells in your body,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.790
and the so-called
germline cells, which

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:06.873
are the egg and the sperm.

00:11:06.873 --> 00:11:09.690
The egg and the sperm are these
highly protected populations

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:13.410
of cells that, in
females, actually come

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:16.110
to be during embryogenesis.

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:18.840
So for all females
out there, you

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:22.440
generate what today I'm going
to refer to as a vault of cells.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:26.730
You have a vault of eggs
that are your germline.

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:32.080
Those eggs all contain all
the chromosomes of your DNA.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:33.620
So it's going to
be-- as most of you

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:37.450
know, there are 23 chromosomes,
and chromosomes exist in pairs.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.530
So the way to think about this
is each pair is one strand,

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.660
and you have 22
so-called autosomes,

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.400
and then you have
one sex chromosome.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.380
The sex chromosome will be
either X or Y. So in a female,

00:11:47.380 --> 00:11:48.760
they have two X chromosomes.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:51.120
So in each one of the
eggs that a woman has

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.640
and that she's had
since she was an embryo

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.500
and that's contained
in this vault,

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:59.520
those eggs are, of course, going
to be very immature at birth.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.960
She hasn't undergone
puberty yet.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.510
And certainly, as an embryo,
she hasn't undergone puberty.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:05.910
And those cells are
going to contain

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.810
23 pairs of chromosomes.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:09.810
This is very important--

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:12.100
23 pairs of chromosomes.

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:16.320
The chromosomes are
essentially the wrapped-up DNA

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.330
that contains all the
genetic information

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:20.190
to create any cell
type in the body

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:23.410
and actually to create an
entirely new individual.

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:26.760
Now, there are 23 pairs of
chromosomes, 22 of which

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.433
are called autosomes.

00:12:28.433 --> 00:12:29.850
If that doesn't
make sense to you,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:32.320
just remember, autosome,
OK, there's 22 of them.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.500
And then there's one
so-called sex chromosome.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.990
The sex chromosomes are either
X or Y. But this is a female,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.500
so she's going to have
23 pairs of chromosomes,

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:44.400
and she's going to have two
X chromosomes for the sex

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:45.118
chromosomes.

00:12:45.118 --> 00:12:47.160
If this is already confusing
to you, don't worry,

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.130
I'll make it very clear how
this all relates to fertility

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:53.010
and how it relates to
chromosomal segregation

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.590
and a bunch of things that I
think maybe you've heard of

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:57.945
and that perhaps
were opaque to you.

00:12:57.945 --> 00:12:59.320
But I promise to
make them clear.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.487
But just understand that,
within each of those eggs,

00:13:01.487 --> 00:13:03.570
they have 23 pairs
of chromosomes.

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.780
And for those of you
that like nomenclature,

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:09.420
I'll tell you that those
cells are considered diploid.

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.160
They're called the
diploid, and that

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:14.010
means that they have 23
pairs of chromosomes,

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:16.890
as distinguished
from cells that are

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:22.210
haploid where there's only one
set of those 23 chromosomes.

00:13:22.210 --> 00:13:25.000
So instead of 23 pairs,
there's only 23 chromosomes.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:28.510
We'll come back to haploid
cells a little bit later.

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:32.460
So when a female is born,
she has all these eggs

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.530
in the reserve, in
this vault, that she'll

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:35.640
have for her entire life.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.260
She's not going
to make any more.

00:13:37.260 --> 00:13:39.240
But they are very,
very immature.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.790
So when a woman is
in embryogenesis,

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.932
she develops these
very immature eggs.

00:13:44.932 --> 00:13:46.890
Today we're also going
to talk about follicles,

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:50.160
and we will be careful
to distinguish follicles

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:50.850
from eggs.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.430
They're often talked about
interchangeably, online

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:56.160
and elsewhere and even by
fertility docs and OB/GYNs.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.330
But right now we're just talking
about the egg cells, the eggs

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.640
themselves, which are cells.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.580
Now, the goal of
fertilization is

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.310
to bring that egg cell
into close enough proximity

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:12.300
that it can be fertilized
by a single sperm cell.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.890
And that sperm cell will
bring 23 chromosomes, as well,

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:17.730
that include--

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:22.380
just as in the female egg, it'll
have 22 autosomes and one sex

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:23.170
chromosome.

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:24.870
And in the male,
that sex chromosome

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:28.910
can either be an X chromosome,
which then would give rise

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.000
to female offspring, or a
Y chromosome, which would

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:33.920
give rise to male offspring.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:36.620
And today we're not talking
about sexual differentiation.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:39.440
That's a topic of a previous
and yet another future episode.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.220
But just to give you a sense
of how X chromosomes and Y

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.110
chromosomes can
actually accomplish

00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:49.730
that sexual differentiation,
both of body and brain.

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:52.800
I'll just mention in two
sentences that, for instance,

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:55.580
if there's a Y chromosome as
opposed to an X chromosome,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:59.760
that Y chromosome contains genes
that suppress, for instance,

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.880
the development of
female genitalia

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:05.458
and thereby give rise
to male genitalia.

00:15:05.458 --> 00:15:07.250
So rather than the
formation of a clitoris,

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.902
it's the formation of a penis.

00:15:08.902 --> 00:15:10.610
And rather than the
formation of ovaries,

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.140
the formation of a testes.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.438
So that's more directed
towards sexual differentiation.

00:15:14.438 --> 00:15:16.230
We're not going to get
into that right now.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.050
We'll get into that
in a future episode.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:21.560
But even if you're
only tracking about 10%

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.150
of what I'm saying right now,
I promise you're doing great.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:25.820
If you're tracking
more than 10%, well,

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:27.320
then you're doing
terrifically well,

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:30.440
because the essence of fertility
and fertilization is to bring

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:37.340
together that haploid cell that
is the sperm that only has 23

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:40.550
chromosomes-- but not
pairs of chromosomes

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:42.470
because that's
the DNA from dad--

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:45.950
together with the egg,
which, as I told you already,

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:47.970
has 23 pairs of chromosomes.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.570
So part of the
fertilization process

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:54.560
has to be to get rid of
one half of those 23 pairs

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:55.250
in the female.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:58.360
You got to get
rid of it, and you

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:00.820
have to get the egg and
the sperm in proximity

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:03.400
so that the egg can
potentially be fertilized

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:07.240
by the sperm bringing
the DNA, the 23

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:11.530
single strands of
chromosomes from dad,

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:16.130
into a cell that has 23
single strands from mom.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.790
So I realize I'm probably being
a little bit repetitive here,

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:21.340
but I want everyone
to understand this

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:24.550
because it really frames up
fertility and reproduction

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:26.770
in the proper way.

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.650
We've got a cell from
mom, the egg, which

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:32.870
has 23 pairs of chromosomes.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:35.860
We need to get rid of
one set of those pairs

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:37.750
so that there's
only 23 chromosomes.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:40.300
We need to get rid of
half of those chromosomes.

00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:43.540
And then we need to bring
that cell together physically

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:48.130
with the sperm cell
that contains the 23

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.562
chromosomal strands from dad.

00:16:50.562 --> 00:16:52.520
And we need to bring
those together so that you

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:54.860
get 23 chromosomal
pairs from dad

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:58.010
and 23 chromosomal
pairs from mom.

00:16:58.010 --> 00:17:02.210
And in doing so, you
create a cell, which

00:17:02.210 --> 00:17:03.620
then becomes multiple cells.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:05.569
That's going to be the
developing embryo that

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:08.089
has half the genes from mom
and half the genes from dad.

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:09.560
So I hope that's clear.

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.342
That is the biological
logic, which I realize

00:17:12.342 --> 00:17:13.550
is a bit of a tongue twister.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:14.960
But forgive me.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.720
It is the most accurate way
to describe this process.

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:21.589
We're trying to bring
together the 23 single strands

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:23.480
of chromosomes
from dad and the 23

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:27.950
single strands of chromosomes
from mom into the same cell.

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:31.850
Now, that requires a
literal physical contact

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:33.380
and pairing of the two cells.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:37.760
But as I mentioned before,
all these eggs in mom

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:40.580
are sitting in a vault, and
they're very, very immature.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:44.450
So the ovulatory cycle
and the menstrual cycle

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:47.060
are really about
first eliminating

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:51.860
half of the chromosomal pairs
in that 23 sets of chromosomes

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.710
and not getting rid of,
for instance, half--

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:59.420
just going 1 to 11 or 12 to 23.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:00.390
That's not the goal.

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.840
The goal is to have
chromosomes 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:05.600
6, all the way up to
23, but only to have

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.230
half of the chromosomes there
and to bring that cell together

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:11.990
with the sperm cell,
physically, then allow

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:14.990
them to fuse and allow
the chromosomes from dad

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:18.380
and the chromosomes from mom
to fuse within a single cell

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:22.070
and duplicate into cells that
contain half of the chromosomes

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:24.290
from dad and half of the
chromosomes from mom.

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:26.420
That's what the ovulatory
and menstrual cycle

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:27.752
are really all about.

00:18:27.752 --> 00:18:29.210
So when thinking
about it that way,

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:32.090
I'd like to just initiate the
discussion by focusing first

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:35.780
on the female component,
or the egg component,

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:39.650
of fertility and fertilization.

00:18:39.650 --> 00:18:43.310
As I mentioned before, a
female has all the eggs,

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:46.820
albeit very immature eggs, that
she's going to have at the time

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:48.320
that she's born.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:52.340
Now, puberty will
happen at some point

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:55.700
and will allow the ovulatory
and the menstrual cycle

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.095
to commence.

00:18:57.095 --> 00:18:58.970
Now, one question that
you perhaps are asking

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.100
is, what controls
the onset of puberty?

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.053
And there are a number
of different results,

00:19:04.053 --> 00:19:05.720
each of which could
be an entire episode

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:07.040
of a podcast on its own.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:08.540
But I'll just
highlight a few things

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:12.770
that we know about the onset
of menses or menstruation,

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:14.942
or it's sometimes also
called a menarche.

00:19:14.942 --> 00:19:17.150
One thing that you'll notice
about today's discussion

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.460
is that if you were to take
any number of your notes

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:22.280
online and put them
into a search function

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:24.680
that you would see a lot
of different language used

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:25.650
for the same thing.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:26.450
So for instance,
some people will

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:28.730
talk about the egg and the
follicle as the same thing,

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:29.960
even though they are not.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.335
I'll explain the
difference soon.

00:19:31.335 --> 00:19:33.500
Some people will talk about
menses or menstruation

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.190
or menarche as the
exact same thing.

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:37.220
And in fact, they are
not the exact same thing,

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:40.340
but oftentimes these words
are used interchangeably.

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.220
I'll do my best today to not
overload you with nomenclature

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.970
but rather to use the
most commonly used terms

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:49.940
for the different aspects of
fertility and fertilization.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.700
But when it comes to
the onset of puberty,

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:55.010
first of all, most
of you have probably

00:19:55.010 --> 00:19:58.640
heard that the
onset of puberty is

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:00.890
happening much
earlier in females now

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:02.310
than it was some years ago.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:04.130
And in fact, that is the case.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.050
And I'll talk about
some statistics related

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:08.060
to this which are
pretty striking

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:11.740
but don't necessarily point
to anything detrimental.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:14.240
It doesn't necessarily mean
that something bad is happening.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:15.380
What do we know for sure?

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.750
Well, we know that
there are a number

00:20:17.750 --> 00:20:20.300
of signals that come
both through the brain

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.370
and through the body--

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:25.340
and more likely both-- in order
to control the onset of puberty

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:26.270
in females.

00:20:26.270 --> 00:20:29.300
A couple of examples-- the
first is a mechanistic one.

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:33.350
We know, for instance,
that the entire process

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:36.398
of the ovulatory menstrual cycle
is initiated from the brain.

00:20:36.398 --> 00:20:37.940
We're going to get
into this in a lot

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:39.148
more detail in a few minutes.

00:20:39.148 --> 00:20:42.860
But there's a certain number of
hormones and neurotransmitters

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:45.200
that are communicated
from the brain,

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.010
a structure called
the hypothalamus,

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.700
which roughly sits above
the roof of your mouth,

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:53.240
and that communicates
with a gland, an endocrine

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:56.850
or hormone-releasing gland
called the pituitary gland.

00:20:56.850 --> 00:21:01.580
The pituitary gland looks
like a stalk that essentially

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:03.060
extends out of the brain.

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:06.140
It's also located not far
from the roof of your mouth.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.900
And that has two
sort of small marble

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:13.863
or grape-sized protrusions,
the anterior pituitary

00:21:13.863 --> 00:21:15.030
and the posterior pituitary.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:18.290
And they release different
hormones into the bloodstream.

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:21.590
Puberty is in part
controlled by the fact

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:24.560
that, up until
puberty, there are

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:26.540
neurons in the
hypothalamus that release

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:28.970
a neurotransmitter called
GABA, which is inhibitory,

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:32.150
and that prevents the
neurons in the hypothalamus

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:36.350
from releasing a very
important hormone called

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.420
gonadotropin-releasing
hormone, or GnRH.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:41.240
So the first thing I'd
really like everyone

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:43.490
to know and commit to
memory today is very easy--

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:46.950
GnRH stands for
gonadotropin-releasing hormone.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.890
This comes from
the brain and will

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:52.370
communicate to the pituitary
to release certain hormones.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:56.330
Prior to puberty, in
both males and females,

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:59.540
there are neurons in the brain
that are actively suppressing

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:01.490
the neurons that release GnRH.

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.925
It's like no puberty,
no puberty, no puberty.

00:22:03.925 --> 00:22:04.942
You can't have puberty.

00:22:04.942 --> 00:22:05.900
You can't have puberty.

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:09.850
And in fact, those cells are
releasing this neurotransmitter

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:11.350
called GABA because
it's inhibitory.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.800
It prevents the firing
of those neurons.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:15.950
So puberty is
actively suppressed up

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:17.060
until a certain point.

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:20.210
It's also actively suppressed,
at least in some species and we

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:24.350
think at least partially in
humans, by the tonic release--

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:27.050
that means the ongoing
release, around the clock--

00:22:27.050 --> 00:22:29.480
of a hormone called melatonin.

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:32.540
Later in life-- in
fact, after puberty--

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:37.100
melatonin will be secreted only
in the dark phase of each night

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:39.270
and around the time
that one goes to sleep.

00:22:39.270 --> 00:22:41.960
But in children
and in particular

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.000
in children prior to
puberty, melatonin

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:46.670
is released more
or less constantly.

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:48.737
Now, melatonin isn't
the only source

00:22:48.737 --> 00:22:49.820
of suppression of puberty.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:53.150
It's also these neural
mechanisms involving GABA.

00:22:53.150 --> 00:22:56.390
But it is certainly a
great candidate for one

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:58.400
of the reasons why
puberty doesn't generally

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.530
tend to happen at,
say, age four age five.

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:03.740
That would be very unusual.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:07.490
Another component of
suppression of puberty

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:11.090
is that typically in children
they have relatively low body

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:11.850
fat stores.

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:12.810
Why is this important?

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:15.860
Well, we know that one of
the things that can trigger

00:23:15.860 --> 00:23:18.770
the onset of puberty-- in
particular in females--

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:22.250
is that when enough
body fat accumulates,

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:25.640
that body fat releases
a hormone called leptin,

00:23:25.640 --> 00:23:28.550
and that hormone leptin
travels in the bloodstream,

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.310
across the blood-brain barrier,
and goes to the hypothalamus

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:32.990
and can trigger the
onset of puberty

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:35.990
by activating the
neurons that release

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:37.710
gonadotropin-releasing hormone.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:41.210
So many people believe that
one of the reasons that puberty

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:43.820
is happening earlier
and earlier in females

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:48.050
is because of the accumulation
of more body fat at younger

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:52.790
ages than was observed 30 or
40 and certainly 100 years ago.

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:55.700
Now, I can already imagine a
number of people are thinking,

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.050
oh, this must relate
to the obesity crisis.

00:23:58.050 --> 00:23:59.810
And indeed, there is
a crisis of obesity.

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:04.940
Obesity is something that is
causing all sorts of problems

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.940
with people's health at
various levels, brain and body,

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:09.740
and that is far
more frequent today

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:11.760
than it was even 20 years ago.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:13.550
So it is indeed a
crisis because it

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.790
has enormous detrimental
effects for so many aspects

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:20.270
of brain and body
health and longevity.

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:22.807
But this whole process of
thinking about body fat

00:24:22.807 --> 00:24:24.890
signaling leptin to the
hypothalamus and the onset

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:27.240
of puberty doesn't
necessarily have

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:28.490
to do with the obesity crisis.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.520
It might relate, but it could
also relate to, for instance,

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:33.830
improved nutrition,
which is allowing body

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:36.890
fat stores to accumulate maybe
not to the level of obesity

00:24:36.890 --> 00:24:40.100
but to accumulate earlier
and at younger ages

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:42.230
in females, which
is then causing

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:44.390
earlier puberty in females.

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:46.670
To just highlight how
that might be possible,

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:50.630
I want to review some data that
talk about the onset of menses,

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.670
menstruation--
that is, puberty--

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:55.700
in females according to
country and according

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:59.010
to age over the last
100 or more years.

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:00.752
So what are the
general trends in terms

00:25:00.752 --> 00:25:02.210
of the onset of
puberty in females?

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:04.940
Well, that's an
easy one to answer.

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:09.320
Over the last 100 years or
so, the onset of puberty

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:13.880
has been occurring much earlier
with each passing decade.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:17.210
It's really an incredible
set of statistics.

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:18.710
I will provide a
link to these data

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:20.793
since I know a number of
you are listening and not

00:25:20.793 --> 00:25:22.620
just watching on YouTube.

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:25.460
This is from a study in
which the onset of puberty

00:25:25.460 --> 00:25:29.270
has been analyzed from
as early as the 1850s--

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:31.520
in certain countries,
there are data on that--

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.470
out to the 1970s and
in other countries

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:37.550
starting at about 1900,
extending out to about 1990.

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:39.980
These are ongoing
collections of data.

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:42.932
But just to give you a sense
of how the data are falling out

00:25:42.932 --> 00:25:44.390
in a couple of
different countries,

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.100
just to give you a flavor--

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:50.210
but for those of you listening
and for those of you watching,

00:25:50.210 --> 00:25:52.430
the essence of all
of these findings

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:54.890
is that puberty is
happening much, much earlier

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:56.150
with each passing decade.

00:25:56.150 --> 00:26:02.280
So for instance, in the United
States, around 1900 or 1903,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:06.140
the average age of menarche,
the onset of puberty, in females

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:11.840
was about 14 years old, whereas
in 1990, the average age is 11.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.080
So that's a pretty
significant, we can say,

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:17.610
acceleration of the
onset of puberty.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.080
Now, of course,
these are averages.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:20.550
So there will be exceptions.

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:22.550
There's a distribution of data.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:25.220
Today, still, there will
be young females who

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:28.280
will undergo puberty at age
11 or 10 or maybe even 9

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:31.670
and others who will undergo
puberty at age 13, 14, maybe

00:26:31.670 --> 00:26:33.440
even 16 or 17.

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:36.060
However, if we look
at, for instance,

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.570
the data from Norway, which
dates back quite far-- they

00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:42.140
have excellent record-keeping--
to 1850, what we see

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:46.730
is that the average age of the
onset of female puberty in 1850

00:26:46.730 --> 00:26:52.330
in Norway was 17 years
old, whereas in 1970, it's

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:53.690
13 years old.

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:56.110
So this is a
dramatic acceleration

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:57.357
of the onset of puberty.

00:26:57.357 --> 00:26:59.690
And you see a similar trend
in other countries, as well.

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:03.070
So if we were to look,
for instance, in the UK,

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:05.680
they have a smaller data
set, meaning it only

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:07.810
extends back to about 1940.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:11.980
But the average age of the onset
of puberty in the UK in 1940

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:13.690
was 13 and 1/2 years old.

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:15.520
Again, this is just for females.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.460
And in 1970, it
was closer to 13,

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.542
with a trend towards
declining even further.

00:27:20.542 --> 00:27:22.000
Unfortunately, they
didn't continue

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:24.760
to collect data out to 2022.

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.710
And as a final point, if we
were to look at, for instance,

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:31.060
in Germany and Finland,
the average onset

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:34.900
of puberty in 1870 was
16 and 1/2 years old.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:38.240
By 1940, it was down to
13 and 1/2 years old.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:42.310
So all of these data have
borne out over and over again,

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:44.680
regardless of location in the
world, which is important,

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:46.540
because when you start to
think about the obesity crisis,

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.910
you can say, well, that's
mainly in developed countries,

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:51.130
believe it or not-- or
perhaps not surprisingly.

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:53.570
And maybe it has to do
with the obesity crisis.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:56.200
And yet I don't think we
can conclude that at all.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:58.330
Something is happening, however.

00:27:58.330 --> 00:28:01.180
It could be increased
body fat stores

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:02.750
due to overeating and obesity.

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:05.410
However, it could also
be-- unrelated to obesity,

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.810
it could be, for
instance, improved

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:11.530
nutrition and the availability
of quality nutrition, which

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:13.960
can signal the
maturation of the brain

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:16.960
and body mechanisms that
trigger the onset of puberty,

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:18.820
ovulatory cycle,
and menstruation.

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:21.370
So we want to be very careful
about leaping to conclusions

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:24.040
about what these trends mean,
but the trends themselves

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:25.630
are very, very apparent.

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:27.430
And as a final
point, I should also

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:29.980
mention that there are a
number of different behavioral

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:32.920
and psychosocial, as they're
called, interactions that

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:34.640
can influence puberty as well.

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:37.610
This has been most strikingly
observed in animals.

00:28:37.610 --> 00:28:39.370
And so I don't want
anyone to be alarmed

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:42.310
or to leap to any great
conclusions about the onset

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:44.798
of timing of puberty in
humans, but I'd be remiss

00:28:44.798 --> 00:28:46.840
if I didn't tell you about
a certain result which

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:52.630
shows that if a young female
is exposed to the odor--

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.160
not necessarily the pheromones.

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:56.770
There's a distinction between
odors that we perceive

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:59.820
and pheromones, which
are subconscious.

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:03.207
We don't actively perceive, but
that can impact our biology,

00:29:03.207 --> 00:29:05.540
and pheromones effects in
humans are very controversial.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:08.560
But we know, for instance, that
if you take a female animal--

00:29:08.560 --> 00:29:10.630
and there's some
evidence from humans

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:13.600
that if you take a
young prepubertal female

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:19.450
and you expose her to the scent
of a reproductively-competent

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:25.300
male for a series of days,
but maybe even as short

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:29.020
as a few hours,
and she is also not

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:32.590
regularly being exposed to
the scent of her father,

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:34.900
that she can undergo
puberty earlier.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:35.740
That's right.

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.720
There is something
about the odor

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:42.460
and/or pheromones, or
perhaps something else, that

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:47.730
occurs when a young prepubertal
female has a father that she's

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:48.730
in regular contact with.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:50.938
He wouldn't necessarily have
to live at home but that

00:29:50.938 --> 00:29:54.460
is around a lot that
his smell is registered

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:56.710
by her biological systems.

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:58.990
That-- I don't want
to say protects

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:02.140
because it kind of skews the
valence of the conversation,

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:08.390
but that offsets or buffers
the otherwise observed effect,

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:11.860
which is that the scent of a
reproductively-competent male,

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:14.920
if it's present often enough
or perhaps intensely enough,

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.440
that it can trigger the onset
of puberty in that female.

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:22.090
In other words, the scent of
a male that is not the father

00:30:22.090 --> 00:30:25.120
and we think also that is not
biologically related to her

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:27.850
can trigger earlier
onset of puberty.

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:32.350
And that effect can at
least be partially buffered

00:30:32.350 --> 00:30:36.040
by her being in the
presence of the scent

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:37.990
from her biological father.

00:30:37.990 --> 00:30:40.000
Now, some of you are
probably already leaping

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:41.542
to conclusions about
what this means.

00:30:41.542 --> 00:30:43.593
Should you not
allow your daughter

00:30:43.593 --> 00:30:45.010
to be exposed to
any males who are

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:47.050
of reproductive age, et cetera?

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:48.635
That's certainly
not what I'm saying.

00:30:48.635 --> 00:30:50.260
There's a huge number
of considerations

00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:52.780
that go into that
calculation for everybody

00:30:52.780 --> 00:30:54.520
and circumstances, et cetera.

00:30:54.520 --> 00:31:00.970
But the point is that the odors
of individuals, both related--

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:05.410
in particular, closely related--
and non-related individuals,

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:10.597
can shape the neural systems
and the hormone systems that

00:31:10.597 --> 00:31:12.430
can trigger the onset
of puberty or suppress

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:13.540
the onset of puberty.

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:15.370
So whether or not we're
talking about onset

00:31:15.370 --> 00:31:17.050
of puberty at this
age or that age

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:20.140
and whether or not
biologically-related male

00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:22.240
or non-biologically-related
male scents around,

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:25.090
et cetera, the thing I
want everyone to know

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:27.850
is that at some point
during development,

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:32.770
typically nowadays between
the ages of 11 and 15

00:31:32.770 --> 00:31:36.550
or so-- again, there's
variability there.

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:39.490
The suppression of
gonadotropin-releasing hormone

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:41.770
released from the
hypothalamus is removed,

00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:44.470
and then
gonadotropin-releasing hormone

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:47.110
can activate cells
within the pituitary.

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:48.610
And if you really
want to know, it's

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:50.770
the anterior pituitary
in particular.

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:53.300
And then the anterior
pituitary gland,

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:57.250
which sits at and kind of
bridges the brain and the body

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:01.270
because it allows the release of
hormones into the bloodstream,

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.520
that anterior pituitary
is going to release

00:32:03.520 --> 00:32:06.520
two key hormones that everyone
should know the name of

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:07.460
and what they do.

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:09.550
And when I say everyone,
I mean males and females

00:32:09.550 --> 00:32:11.200
need to know about
these hormones

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:14.090
because they have an active
role in both males and females.

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:16.330
And of course, you
should want to know

00:32:16.330 --> 00:32:19.150
and should know about
the biology of everyone

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:21.773
on the planet, in my
opinion, because it tells you

00:32:21.773 --> 00:32:23.440
a lot more about
humans than if you just

00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:24.820
focus on your own biology.

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:26.830
But those two
hormones are called

00:32:26.830 --> 00:32:31.120
luteinizing hormone,
which is abbreviated LH,

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:34.030
and follicle-stimulating
hormone, which

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:35.890
is abbreviated FSH.

00:32:35.890 --> 00:32:38.830
So the simple picture that
you need to have in your mind

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:41.230
is gonadotropin-releasing
hormone from the brain,

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:43.450
from the hypothalamus
in particular,

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:46.090
is causing the
release of luteinizing

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:50.920
hormone and follicle-stimulating
hormone, GnRH stimulates

00:32:50.920 --> 00:32:53.890
LH, luteinizing hormone, and
follicle-stimulating hormone,

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:55.090
FSH.

00:32:55.090 --> 00:32:59.380
LH and FSH travel in the blood
and can access all the cells

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:00.380
and tissues of the body.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:04.330
This is one of the incredible
things about hormones is that

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:08.830
many hormones-- and LH and FSH
are included in this group--

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:12.550
can travel into cells,
and they can actually

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:15.040
change the genetic
expression of those cells.

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:16.900
They can change which
genes are turned on

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.192
and which genes are turned off.

00:33:18.192 --> 00:33:21.367
And they can also attach to
the surface of those cells

00:33:21.367 --> 00:33:23.450
and make those cells take
on different properties.

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:24.742
So they can mature those cells.

00:33:24.742 --> 00:33:27.310
So for instance, a good
example of this outside

00:33:27.310 --> 00:33:28.990
of the context we've
been talking about

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:32.365
is the hormone testosterone
can travel to the hair follicle

00:33:32.365 --> 00:33:34.990
and can stimulate changes in the
genes of the cells of the hair

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:37.120
follicle that can
make hair grow.

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:39.190
A different hormone,
estrogen, can

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:41.800
travel to the cells
of the breast tissue

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:44.290
and activate genes that control
enlargement of the cells

00:33:44.290 --> 00:33:45.280
of the breast tissue.

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:48.520
Prolactin, a different hormone,
can travel to the mammary ducts

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:52.090
and control the production
and the secretion of milk.

00:33:52.090 --> 00:33:55.550
And in males, that can actually
happen, in certain cases,

00:33:55.550 --> 00:33:56.530
although it's rare.

00:33:56.530 --> 00:33:59.110
But prolactin can also
travel to areas of the brain

00:33:59.110 --> 00:34:01.090
that control libido,
for instance.

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:03.340
And just so you'll
never forget it,

00:34:03.340 --> 00:34:05.440
males' elevated
levels of prolactin

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:07.540
are actually what set
the refractory period

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:09.639
after ejaculation
and prevent erection

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:10.940
for some period of time.

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:13.960
So you'll never
forget prolactin.

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:16.090
The point being that
different hormones

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:17.889
have different effects
on different cells,

00:34:17.889 --> 00:34:19.366
depending on what
cells those are.

00:34:19.366 --> 00:34:21.699
Estrogen or estradiol is going
to have different effects

00:34:21.699 --> 00:34:24.370
on the breast tissue
than it would on skin,

00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:25.840
although as effects on both.

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:29.710
Similarly, when LH and
FSH, luteinizing hormone

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:32.830
and follicle-stimulating
hormone, travel in the blood

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:36.820
to the gonad and the
gonad is an ovary,

00:34:36.820 --> 00:34:39.860
it will have a certain
set of consequences.

00:34:39.860 --> 00:34:42.480
And when luteinizing hormone
and follicle-stimulating hormone

00:34:42.480 --> 00:34:44.909
travel in the blood to
a gonad and that gonad

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:47.820
happens to be a teste, then
it will have a different set

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:49.690
of biological implications.

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:52.080
So let's focus now
on what happens when

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:54.989
LH and FSH arrive at the ovary.

00:34:54.989 --> 00:34:59.190
And let's assume now that we're
talking about a female who

00:34:59.190 --> 00:35:02.880
has already undergone
puberty, or perhaps we

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:05.460
could even frame this in
the context of a female who

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:07.770
is about to undergo puberty.

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:10.650
FSH and LH are now able to
be released because she's

00:35:10.650 --> 00:35:12.120
undergoing puberty.

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:14.820
But the same set of
processes, essentially,

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:19.510
would occur for any point from
puberty onward until menopause,

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:24.180
which is the depletion of that
vault, that ovarian reserve

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:25.680
of all those immature eggs.

