00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:36.360
OK, so we're now going
to talk about ovulation

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.068
and menstruation, and
let's just remember

00:36:38.068 --> 00:36:39.100
what this is all about.

00:36:39.100 --> 00:36:42.390
This is all about creating
the potential for an egg

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.220
to be fertilized,
and that egg needs

00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:48.180
to have half of the
chromosomal pairs, so no pairs,

00:36:48.180 --> 00:36:51.120
but it's got to have 23
chromosomes just from mom.

00:36:51.120 --> 00:36:53.820
And we need to
position that egg so

00:36:53.820 --> 00:36:56.820
that the egg can
be met by the sperm

00:36:56.820 --> 00:36:59.010
and that sperm can
penetrate that egg

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:04.200
and donate its 23 individual
strands of chromosomes

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:07.950
to that egg so that you can
bring together the DNA of dad

00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:09.300
and the DNA of mom.

00:37:09.300 --> 00:37:11.460
So the obligatory
menstrual cycle

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:16.080
occurs when luteinizing hormone
and follicle-stimulating

00:37:16.080 --> 00:37:18.120
hormone have been released.

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:21.810
And the
ovulatory/menstrual cycle--

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:24.683
and here I have to kind of
pick what I want to call it.

00:37:24.683 --> 00:37:26.850
I guess to be really accurate,
we would just call it

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:30.545
the female reproductive cycle,
but that includes underneath it

00:37:30.545 --> 00:37:32.670
both the menstrual cycle,
as it's sometimes called,

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:34.000
and the ovulatory cycle.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:34.950
So you decide.

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:38.250
I'm going to interchangeably
discuss the ovulatory cycle

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:39.470
and the menstrual cycle.

00:37:39.470 --> 00:37:41.220
The problem is, when
you say menstruation,

00:37:41.220 --> 00:37:43.590
people often think about
just the period, the shedding

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.630
of the uterine lining when
fertilization has not occurred.

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:49.350
So if I start saying
ovulatory cycle,

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:52.570
just keep in mind I'm
referring to the entire thing.

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.780
Now, this is probably
also a good opportunity

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:59.370
to say that if you heard that
the ovulatory/menstrual cycle

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:02.850
is 28 days long, that's
true in some cases,

00:38:02.850 --> 00:38:04.420
but that's not always true.

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:07.200
It's, on average, 28 days long.

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:11.490
There are some females for which
the ovulatory cycle will be

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.680
shorter-- it can be
as short as 21 days--

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:18.480
and other females for which
it will be 35 days long.

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.850
Shorter than 21 days
and longer than 35

00:38:20.850 --> 00:38:23.465
days is rare, although
it does occur.

00:38:23.465 --> 00:38:25.590
One of the key things when
thinking about fertility

00:38:25.590 --> 00:38:28.800
is if you talk to OB/GYNs who
are focused on fertility, which

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:30.720
I have in anticipation
of this episode,

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:33.030
they'll tell you
that whether or not

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:36.900
your cycle is 21 days
long or 35 days long

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:40.530
is not as much of an issue
necessarily unless it's

00:38:40.530 --> 00:38:44.100
happening to become much
shorter or much longer

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:45.880
in a kind of erratic way.

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:47.640
So if you're somebody
who's consistently

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:50.190
had 23 day long cycles
and all of a sudden

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:53.010
you're having 30 day long
cycles, that's not necessarily

00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:54.390
an indication of anything bad.

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:58.330
But if it's 21 days one month
and it's 30 days the next month

00:38:58.330 --> 00:39:01.950
and it's 17 days the next
month or even if it's always

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:05.100
falling within that 21
to 35 day long cycle

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:08.040
but it's very variable from
each month or every other month

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:11.605
or so, you probably want
to talk to your OB/GYN

00:39:11.605 --> 00:39:13.980
because that could indicate
a number of different things.

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:15.300
Which things could it indicate?

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:17.250
Well, that will become
clear as I spell out

00:39:17.250 --> 00:39:19.350
the biology in a
bit more detail.

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.350
But this idea that the
menstrual cycle/ovulatory cycle

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:24.330
is always 28 days,
that's just false.

00:39:24.330 --> 00:39:25.890
That's just not true.

00:39:25.890 --> 00:39:29.580
I should also mention that there
is a common misconception that

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:32.670
because the average menstrual
cycle is 28 days-- indeed,

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:34.140
the average is 28 days--

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:36.607
and the lunar cycle is 28 days--

00:39:36.607 --> 00:39:38.190
and of course, there
is a real biology

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:39.607
to support the
fact that the lunar

00:39:39.607 --> 00:39:42.140
cycle can't, in fact,
impact certain aspects

00:39:42.140 --> 00:39:43.620
of human behavior.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:45.560
It does, and we'll
talk about lunar cycles

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:46.820
in a future episode.

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:50.180
But there is zero data
to support the idea

00:39:50.180 --> 00:39:53.030
that the menstrual cycle
and the lunar cycle

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:56.120
are linked in any
kind of causal way.

00:39:56.120 --> 00:39:57.500
Sorry to break it to you.

00:39:57.500 --> 00:40:01.407
The lunar cycle and the
tidal cycles at the ocean

00:40:01.407 --> 00:40:03.740
are definitely linked in ways
that are super interesting

00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:05.720
related to the tilt of the
Earth and the pull of gravity

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:07.940
of different planets, and
it's an incredible story

00:40:07.940 --> 00:40:09.020
into itself.

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:11.300
But the lunar cycle and
the menstrual cycle,

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:13.850
despite having some weak
correlation in terms

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:18.410
of their duration or their
so-called periodicity--

00:40:18.410 --> 00:40:20.210
no pun intended--

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:22.880
well, there's no causal
relationship whatsoever

00:40:22.880 --> 00:40:25.500
between the lunar cycle
and the menstrual cycle.

00:40:25.500 --> 00:40:28.880
If any of you are
aware of any real data

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:30.620
that conflicts with
what I just said,

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:33.440
please put that in the
comment section on YouTube.

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:36.440
But this is pretty well
established as far as I know.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:42.110
OK, so we need to bring together
the so-called haploid contents,

00:40:42.110 --> 00:40:45.620
the 23 individual strands
of chromosomes from the egg,

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.260
to a place and a position
where it could potentially

00:40:48.260 --> 00:40:49.440
be fertilized by the male.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:50.570
So what happens?

00:40:50.570 --> 00:40:52.850
Luteinizing hormone and
follicle-stimulating hormone

00:40:52.850 --> 00:40:54.587
travel to the ovary.

00:40:54.587 --> 00:40:56.420
These hormones are able
to access the ovary.

00:40:56.420 --> 00:40:58.910
There's a lot of blood
supply to the ovary.

00:40:58.910 --> 00:41:03.690
And FSH and LH
arrive at the ovary.

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:06.680
The ovary has this vault,
this ovarian reserve

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:07.685
of immature cells.

00:41:07.685 --> 00:41:09.560
They reside within what
are called follicles.

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:12.650
The follicles are little
spherical packages

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:16.580
that can potentially provide a
nice environment for those eggs

00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:17.780
to mature.

00:41:17.780 --> 00:41:23.330
And when FSH, in particular,
arrives at the ovary,

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:25.880
a small number of
those follicles

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:30.540
will split off from the reserve,
they will exit the vault,

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:32.900
and they will
undergo maturation.

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:37.490
And the key player here is
follicle-stimulating hormone.

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:41.750
And the first 14 days of the
menstrual/ovulatory cycle

00:41:41.750 --> 00:41:44.840
is referred to as the
follicular phase because

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.720
of this relationship between
FSH triggering the maturation

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:49.820
of a subset of follicles.

00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:53.720
Now, typically in the
context of a 28 day or so

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:55.910
ovulatory/menstrual
cycle, day one

00:41:55.910 --> 00:41:58.340
is designated as the
first day of the period,

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:00.530
of the shedding of
the uterine lining

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:04.760
from the previous ovulatory
menstrual cycle in which

00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:07.170
fertilization did not occur.

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:10.910
So day one is when
the period initiates.

00:42:10.910 --> 00:42:13.532
It is days 1 through
14, approximately--

00:42:13.532 --> 00:42:14.990
because here we're
just considering

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:17.100
the average of a 28-day
cycle, but it could be longer.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:17.933
It could be shorter.

00:42:17.933 --> 00:42:21.230
But the first half of that cycle
is the so-called follicular

00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:24.470
phase, FSH, has
triggered the departure

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:27.710
of a subset of these follicles
that contain immature eggs.

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:30.680
And it is triggering the
maturation of those eggs.

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:33.980
Luteinizing hormone is
also present, but also

00:42:33.980 --> 00:42:35.433
at relatively low levels.

00:42:35.433 --> 00:42:37.850
And it's during the first half
of this ovulatory menstrual

00:42:37.850 --> 00:42:41.480
cycle that the main goal is to
get those follicles to mature.

00:42:41.480 --> 00:42:44.720
So inside of those follicles,
the egg is developing.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:45.650
It's growing.

00:42:45.650 --> 00:42:47.360
It's maturing.

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:51.300
And in doing so, it's also
making its own hormones.

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:54.020
This, I think, is one of
the most elegant aspects

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:57.102
of the ovulatory menstrual
cycle that, in a few minutes,

00:42:57.102 --> 00:42:59.310
you'll learn about something
which still to this day,

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:01.610
even though I've known about
this stuff for decades now

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:05.210
because of my training, still
just blows my mind that you

00:43:05.210 --> 00:43:07.880
have one hormone,
follicle-stimulating hormone,

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:10.420
triggering the
maturation of some eggs

00:43:10.420 --> 00:43:12.920
inside of some follicles and
then those follicles themselves

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:17.180
making another hormone
that furthers the process

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:21.468
and then soon, as you'll learn,
create a hormone to trigger

00:43:21.468 --> 00:43:22.760
the second half of the process.

00:43:22.760 --> 00:43:26.660
Just a beautiful symphony of
expression of different genes

00:43:26.660 --> 00:43:28.550
and different hormones
to make everything

00:43:28.550 --> 00:43:30.170
work as optimally as possible.

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:33.860
So as these different
follicles mature, somehow--

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:35.960
and we still don't
know exactly how--

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:38.900
one of those follicles
containing an egg

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:40.790
gets selected.

00:43:40.790 --> 00:43:42.950
It's either because
it matures the fastest

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:45.920
or there's something
about it that

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:48.350
is still not
completely understood

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:50.420
that allows it to be selected.

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:53.330
And all the other
follicles that are maturing

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:54.860
degenerate and die.

00:43:54.860 --> 00:43:55.610
And they're gone.

00:43:55.610 --> 00:43:57.950
They don't go back into
the ovarian reserve.

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:01.190
They are now depleted
from that bank account

00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:03.230
that is the ovarian reserve.

00:44:03.230 --> 00:44:04.700
They die off.

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:08.420
But that single egg
that, keep in mind,

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:11.630
contains 23 pairs
of chromosomes--

00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:15.140
we haven't gotten rid
of one half of those 23

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:16.730
sets of chromosomes yet.

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:18.800
But that one will
continue to mature.

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:23.150
And then, at some
point, that egg

00:44:23.150 --> 00:44:28.040
will start to undergo a process
in which those chromosomes are

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:31.400
pulled apart by
little components

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:33.530
within the egg called spindles.

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:35.480
They literally have
a physical pulling

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:36.570
of the chromosomes apart.

00:44:36.570 --> 00:44:40.260
So now those 23 pairs are no
longer attached to one another

00:44:40.260 --> 00:44:41.760
at the middle like
they were before,

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:44.280
like two beads of strings--

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:47.520
or I should say,
23 short strands

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:51.600
of beads that were at once
connected to one another

00:44:51.600 --> 00:44:53.340
now are pulled apart
so that you have

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:56.400
23 chromosomes on
each side, but they're

00:44:56.400 --> 00:44:58.900
pulled apart from one another.

00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:02.760
So that diploid
cell is now starting

00:45:02.760 --> 00:45:05.860
to become a cell in which
half of the chromosomes,

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:08.610
half of those 23
pairs, are physically

00:45:08.610 --> 00:45:10.110
pulled away from the others.

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:13.500
And then the egg actually
starts to form its own

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:15.120
what we call an
involution of membrane

00:45:15.120 --> 00:45:20.490
around those 23 pairs, one set
of them, and encapsulates them.

00:45:20.490 --> 00:45:22.650
So you sort of got
an egg with two parts

00:45:22.650 --> 00:45:24.237
where the two sets
of chromosomes,

00:45:24.237 --> 00:45:26.070
two sets of 23 chromosomes,
are now separate

00:45:26.070 --> 00:45:28.170
from one another
inside of the egg.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:32.010
And then one of those actually
gets ejected from the egg,

00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:34.350
and the name of that
thing that gets ejected--

00:45:34.350 --> 00:45:37.290
it's sort of like a little
Hubble pod is how I imagine it,

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:41.220
you know, from Star Wars or from
any kind of Space Odyssey movie

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:43.110
where some thing
is ready to happen.

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:45.060
A little Hubble pod
shoots out of the ship.

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:49.710
Well, that 23 pairs is
now ejected from the egg.

00:45:49.710 --> 00:45:50.880
It's called the polar body.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:52.410
And that's going to degenerate.

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:53.670
It's going to go away.

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:56.730
And in doing so, take the
egg cell, which was once

00:45:56.730 --> 00:45:59.670
diploid-- it had 23 pairs
of chromosomes-- and making

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:01.020
it haploid.

00:46:01.020 --> 00:46:04.230
And now what you've got,
in ideal circumstances,

00:46:04.230 --> 00:46:07.500
is a beautifully pristine
egg that was selected for

00:46:07.500 --> 00:46:11.702
and has 23 single strands
of chromosomes, 22 autosomes

00:46:11.702 --> 00:46:12.660
and one sex chromosome.

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.450
And that sex chromosome is
going to be an X chromosome

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:19.290
almost with certainty,
because female--

00:46:19.290 --> 00:46:22.540
mother-- is creating that egg.

00:46:22.540 --> 00:46:26.640
So then the egg that contains
just the appropriate 23

00:46:26.640 --> 00:46:29.250
single-stranded
chromosomes is going

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:31.530
to fuse with the wall of
the ovary, and that egg

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:36.000
will be released and will
travel into the Fallopian tube.

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:38.290
Now, we'll get back to
that egg in a few moments.

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:40.650
But that process, which
represents the first half

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:42.450
of the ovulatory
menstrual cycle, again,

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:45.880
was triggered by FSH and to
some extent luteinizing hormone.

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:51.480
But it is the ongoing maturation
of that egg which also causes

00:46:51.480 --> 00:46:55.510
the production of estrogen,
which allows that whole process

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:56.010
to occur.

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:57.220
And you could say, why?

00:46:57.220 --> 00:46:59.767
Well, the answer to the
why is a very important

00:46:59.767 --> 00:47:01.350
biological principle
that we are going

00:47:01.350 --> 00:47:03.558
to return to in a number of
different contexts today,

00:47:03.558 --> 00:47:08.040
both as reference to
female and male fertility.

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:12.550
And the principle is a
so-called negative feedback.

00:47:12.550 --> 00:47:15.720
So when estrogen is present
at relatively low levels

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:19.080
in females in the
ovary, as it is

00:47:19.080 --> 00:47:22.470
during the development of these
eggs, some of that estrogen,

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:24.857
of course, is going
to exit the ovary.

00:47:24.857 --> 00:47:26.440
It's going to go
into the bloodstream.

00:47:26.440 --> 00:47:29.810
And it's going to travel
back to the pituitary.

00:47:29.810 --> 00:47:32.078
Now, the pituitary
can release things

00:47:32.078 --> 00:47:34.120
like follicle-stimulating
hormone and luteinizing

00:47:34.120 --> 00:47:34.720
hormone.

00:47:34.720 --> 00:47:36.400
But the way I'd
like you to think

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:38.860
about the pituitary for
sake of feedback loops

00:47:38.860 --> 00:47:41.020
is that it's sort of
like a thermometer

00:47:41.020 --> 00:47:44.890
that you would put into a pool,
like a backyard pool, that

00:47:44.890 --> 00:47:46.390
is attached to the heater.

00:47:46.390 --> 00:47:50.380
And for instance, if you were
to put a thermometer into a pool

00:47:50.380 --> 00:47:52.900
that you would like
to keep at 70 degrees

00:47:52.900 --> 00:47:55.600
and the temperature of
that pool is 60 degrees,

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.340
well, then that
thermometer ought

00:47:57.340 --> 00:47:59.560
to trigger some
sort of mechanism

00:47:59.560 --> 00:48:01.930
where the pool would heat
up until the temperature

00:48:01.930 --> 00:48:04.120
of the pool hit 70
degrees, and then it

00:48:04.120 --> 00:48:06.903
should trigger that thermometer
to turn off the heating system.

00:48:06.903 --> 00:48:08.320
That's kind of a
negative feedback

00:48:08.320 --> 00:48:10.112
system that would keep
the temperature more

00:48:10.112 --> 00:48:11.650
or less correct.

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:14.780
That's a lot of the way that
the system's related to estrogen

00:48:14.780 --> 00:48:16.780
and also testosterone and
these different things

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:19.155
like luteinizing hormone and
follicle-stimulating hormone

00:48:19.155 --> 00:48:20.020
work as well.

00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:24.350
Typically, when the level
of a hormone is too high,

00:48:24.350 --> 00:48:26.020
then it shuts down
the production

00:48:26.020 --> 00:48:28.300
of the hormones that would
trigger further production

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:28.840
of that hormone.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:29.680
I know that's a mouthful.

00:48:29.680 --> 00:48:30.740
It's a lot to think about.

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:31.820
And some of you are
probably thinking, whoa,

00:48:31.820 --> 00:48:33.460
I'm getting dizzy
now with biology.

00:48:33.460 --> 00:48:35.350
But I promise you, you
can understand this.

00:48:35.350 --> 00:48:38.360
In females, when estrogen
is relatively low--

00:48:38.360 --> 00:48:40.060
but not zero but
is relatively low

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:43.840
during that first follicular
half of the ovulatory cycle--

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:47.260
it actually triggers negative
feedback on LH and FSH

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:49.060
so that not too
much is produced.

00:48:49.060 --> 00:48:51.610
But then just
prior to ovulation,

00:48:51.610 --> 00:48:54.790
the levels of estrogen and the
levels of some other hormones

00:48:54.790 --> 00:48:56.680
from those eggs--

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:59.350
you have the eggs producing
estrogen themselves--

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:01.180
gets high enough
that it actually

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:04.790
triggers a positive feedback
loop on the pituitary.

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:07.720
So the pituitary is
essentially observing

00:49:07.720 --> 00:49:09.820
the amount of estrogen in
the bloodstream produced

00:49:09.820 --> 00:49:11.650
by the ovary, and the
amount of estrogen

00:49:11.650 --> 00:49:14.350
towards the end of the second
half of the menstrual cycle

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:18.160
has increased and triggers
a positive feedback loop.

00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:22.180
It triggers the pituitary
to release more FSH and LH,

00:49:22.180 --> 00:49:26.890
and that helps trigger
ovulation, that deployment

00:49:26.890 --> 00:49:29.920
or the release of that
one mature proper selected

00:49:29.920 --> 00:49:33.460
egg that's haploid with the 23
individual pairs of chromosomes

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:35.665
into the Fallopian tube.

00:49:35.665 --> 00:49:37.540
So let's just back up
really quickly and just

00:49:37.540 --> 00:49:38.957
kind of summarize
what's happened.

00:49:38.957 --> 00:49:41.493
Gonadotropin-releasing
hormone from the hypothalamus

00:49:41.493 --> 00:49:43.660
triggers the release of
follicle-stimulating hormone

00:49:43.660 --> 00:49:44.800
and luteinizing hormone.

00:49:44.800 --> 00:49:46.720
That travels to the
ovary-- triggers

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:50.260
the release of a subset
of immature follicles

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:51.850
with immature eggs.

00:49:51.850 --> 00:49:55.030
Those immature follicles
and immature eggs

00:49:55.030 --> 00:49:58.180
start to mature,
start to grow because

00:49:58.180 --> 00:50:00.610
of the presence of
follicle-stimulating hormone.

00:50:00.610 --> 00:50:04.360
The growth of those eggs
themselves increases estrogen.

00:50:04.360 --> 00:50:06.920
As the estrogen starts to
accumulate in the environment,

00:50:06.920 --> 00:50:09.220
some of that travels
back to the pituitary.

00:50:09.220 --> 00:50:12.670
And when levels of estrogen
arriving at the pituitary

00:50:12.670 --> 00:50:15.393
are relatively
low, the pituitary

00:50:15.393 --> 00:50:16.810
says, oh, we don't
need to release

00:50:16.810 --> 00:50:19.210
any more follicle-stimulating
and luteinizing hormone.

00:50:19.210 --> 00:50:21.640
However, at some point
just prior to ovulation,

00:50:21.640 --> 00:50:24.280
enough estrogen has been
produced by that one

00:50:24.280 --> 00:50:26.560
single selected
mature egg and some

00:50:26.560 --> 00:50:29.740
of the other follicles around
it that were maturing but then

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:33.860
since died off that the estrogen
triggers a positive feedback

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:34.360
loop.

00:50:34.360 --> 00:50:37.150
The pituitary says,
OK, and releases

00:50:37.150 --> 00:50:38.680
more follicle-stimulating
hormone

00:50:38.680 --> 00:50:39.790
and luteinizing hormone.

00:50:39.790 --> 00:50:44.500
And bam, the egg, which has
the proper genetic components,

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:48.310
sets off out of the ovary
and into the Fallopian tube.

00:50:48.310 --> 00:50:51.790
So-called ovulation has begun.

00:50:51.790 --> 00:50:53.710
That itself, what
I just described,

00:50:53.710 --> 00:50:56.902
constitutes the first half of
the ovulatory/menstrual cycle,

00:50:56.902 --> 00:50:58.360
which we call the
follicular phase.

00:50:58.360 --> 00:51:01.360
And it's marked by the presence
of FSH and some other things,

00:51:01.360 --> 00:51:04.300
but we can really think
about it as marked by FSH

00:51:04.300 --> 00:51:08.860
from the pituitary and by
estrogen, or estradiol, made

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:10.280
within the ovary.

00:51:10.280 --> 00:51:13.520
Then comes the second half of
the ovulatory/menstrual cycle,

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:16.480
which I personally think is
one of the coolest mechanisms

00:51:16.480 --> 00:51:19.210
in all of biology,
which is that--

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:21.010
remember the
follicle that housed

00:51:21.010 --> 00:51:23.230
that one egg that
was the selected

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:24.880
egg that became the mature egg?

00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:27.760
And that follicle, which
no longer contains the egg

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:29.530
because the egg took
off and ovulated,

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:30.880
is called the corpus luteum.

00:51:30.880 --> 00:51:34.720
And the corpus luteum starts
making three hormones, which

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:36.628
include estradiol, I
think called inhibin,

00:51:36.628 --> 00:51:38.920
but the most important hormone,
the one that you really

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:40.600
need to know about,
is that it starts

00:51:40.600 --> 00:51:44.500
producing very high
levels of progesterone.

00:51:44.500 --> 00:51:47.890
Progesterone levels
start to increase

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:50.350
about the time of
ovulation, although just

00:51:50.350 --> 00:51:51.530
prior to ovulation.

00:51:51.530 --> 00:51:56.240
And over the next second
half of the ovulatory cycle--

00:51:56.240 --> 00:51:57.977
so about 14 days if
it's a 28-day cycle,

00:51:57.977 --> 00:51:59.810
a little bit longer or
a little bit shorter,

00:51:59.810 --> 00:52:01.185
depending on the
length of cycle.

00:52:01.185 --> 00:52:03.040
Levels of progesterone
in the second half

00:52:03.040 --> 00:52:08.200
of the ovulatory cycle are
going to increase by 1,400 fold

00:52:08.200 --> 00:52:10.780
compared to what they
were in the first half

00:52:10.780 --> 00:52:12.740
of the ovulatory cycle.

00:52:12.740 --> 00:52:14.290
So again, if we
were to characterize

00:52:14.290 --> 00:52:18.850
the menstrual/ovulatory cycle
in broad strokes, what we would

00:52:18.850 --> 00:52:22.690
say is that FSH
and estrogen mark

00:52:22.690 --> 00:52:26.710
the initial part the first half,
the so-called follicular phase,

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:30.970
and that the estrogen and
FSH set in motion ovulation,

00:52:30.970 --> 00:52:33.700
and they prime the
system for the production

00:52:33.700 --> 00:52:36.670
of a corpus luteum, which
produces progesterone.

00:52:36.670 --> 00:52:38.740
And the second
half of all of this

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:41.030
is called the luteal phase.

00:52:41.030 --> 00:52:43.210
The second half of the
ovulatory/menstrual cycle

00:52:43.210 --> 00:52:46.600
is the luteal phase
because of corpus luteum,

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.560
this otherwise discarded tissue
that produces progesterone.

00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:52.210
What does progesterone do?

00:52:52.210 --> 00:52:56.710
Well, progesterone impacts
the uterine lining,

00:52:56.710 --> 00:52:58.810
so-called endometrium
or the lining,

00:52:58.810 --> 00:53:03.220
the mucous lining of the uterus
where that egg that's ovulated

00:53:03.220 --> 00:53:06.700
is potentially going to
implant if it's fertilized.

00:53:06.700 --> 00:53:09.580
And so in a kind of
perfect way-- or I

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:12.400
should say, in a
seemingly perfect way--

00:53:12.400 --> 00:53:13.840
the egg is off on its way.

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:15.980
It might get fertilized.

00:53:15.980 --> 00:53:19.760
The remnants of the compartment
that let go of that egg

00:53:19.760 --> 00:53:23.930
produce a hormone that then
prepares the endometrial lining

00:53:23.930 --> 00:53:27.320
of the uterus for the potential
implantation of that egg.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:30.170
It's basically making the
bed for the fertilized egg

00:53:30.170 --> 00:53:33.770
to potentially embed in,
to implant in, and then

00:53:33.770 --> 00:53:37.040
achieve all the nourishment that
it needs to grow, eventually,

00:53:37.040 --> 00:53:39.380
into a healthy embryo and child.

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:42.230
Just an amazing set of
biological mechanisms,

00:53:42.230 --> 00:53:45.920
if you ask me, because
what you're observing here

00:53:45.920 --> 00:53:48.170
is an incredible
economy of function

00:53:48.170 --> 00:53:50.690
whereby the same
cellular components that

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:52.480
are producing the egg,
well, some of them

00:53:52.480 --> 00:53:54.230
are being discarded,
but they're not being

00:53:54.230 --> 00:53:56.138
discarded without purpose.

00:53:56.138 --> 00:53:57.680
They're being
discarded in a way that

00:53:57.680 --> 00:54:01.040
triggers the onset of
hormonal expression

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:02.480
that then prepares
the fertilized

00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:05.150
egg to be in an enriched
environment in which it

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:06.000
can thrive.

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:07.670
Now, I realize that
was a lot of detail.

00:54:07.670 --> 00:54:09.590
But we have a couple
of key themes.

00:54:09.590 --> 00:54:11.570
We've got the
hypothalamus, GnRH.

00:54:11.570 --> 00:54:14.090
We've got the pituitary
with LH and FSH,

00:54:14.090 --> 00:54:16.560
and those hormones
travel to the ovary.

00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:20.990
The ovary has eggs in a
vault, basically immature eggs

00:54:20.990 --> 00:54:24.050
in a vault. Some of those
are activated by the presence

00:54:24.050 --> 00:54:26.090
of FSH and LH each month.

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:30.200
And one of those eggs will
be selected and will ovulate.

00:54:30.200 --> 00:54:32.060
The remnants of the
follicle and egg

00:54:32.060 --> 00:54:35.030
that are not selected, the
chromosomes that you don't need

00:54:35.030 --> 00:54:36.830
disappear in the polar body.

00:54:36.830 --> 00:54:40.460
And the corpus luteum gives
rise to progesterone and sets

00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:44.070
in motion the second half of
the ovulatory menstrual cycle,

00:54:44.070 --> 00:54:46.670
which is the luteal phase,
which is essentially

00:54:46.670 --> 00:54:48.500
the potential for
that fertilized egg

00:54:48.500 --> 00:54:50.632
to embed in a nice,
nourishing environment.

00:54:50.632 --> 00:54:52.340
And of course, we
should all be thinking,

00:54:52.340 --> 00:54:54.650
if the egg is fertilized
and then it lays down

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:56.720
in the nice, comfy
uterine lining

00:54:56.720 --> 00:54:59.120
that's been prepared
by progesterone

00:54:59.120 --> 00:55:02.780
in the corpus luteum, well,
then everything's fine and good.

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:04.517
But what if fertilization
doesn't occur?

00:55:04.517 --> 00:55:07.100
Well, we all know what happens
if fertilization doesn't occur.

00:55:07.100 --> 00:55:09.980
If fertilization does not
occur for whatever reason,

00:55:09.980 --> 00:55:12.752
that uterine lining
is going to shed.

00:55:12.752 --> 00:55:14.960
And that's actually what's
referred to as the period.

00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:17.570
It's the actual removal--

00:55:17.570 --> 00:55:19.220
or the departure, rather--

00:55:19.220 --> 00:55:23.060
of the thickened
endometrium lining

00:55:23.060 --> 00:55:28.420
of the uterus when
fertilization has not occurred.

00:55:28.420 --> 00:55:30.130
And of course, if
that happens, we

00:55:30.130 --> 00:55:32.440
need another ovulatory
menstrual cycle.

00:55:32.440 --> 00:55:33.440
So how does that happen?

00:55:33.440 --> 00:55:36.940
Well, the hormone inhibin is
also made by the corpus luteum

00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:41.320
and doesn't go quite as high
as the hormone progesterone.

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:44.920
But it kind of tracks that
increase in progesterone

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:47.860
that occurs in the second
half of the ovulatory cycle.

00:55:47.860 --> 00:55:50.980
But then, if fertilization
does not occur,

00:55:50.980 --> 00:55:53.290
inhibin levels start to drop.

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:55.960
And what I haven't told
you is what inhibin does.

00:55:55.960 --> 00:55:59.710
Inhibin, in concert with
other hormones like estrogen,

00:55:59.710 --> 00:56:03.460
feed back to the hypothalamus
and prevent the further release

00:56:03.460 --> 00:56:05.920
of follicle-stimulating hormone
and luteinizing hormone.

00:56:05.920 --> 00:56:09.820
If you have an egg that gets
fertilized and can implant,

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:13.420
well, then you don't
want more eggs to mature.

00:56:13.420 --> 00:56:15.520
You want to hold on to
the ones in the vault.

00:56:15.520 --> 00:56:17.560
You don't want them to mature.

00:56:17.560 --> 00:56:19.768
And hormones like
inhibin and, again,

00:56:19.768 --> 00:56:21.310
working with other
hormones are going

00:56:21.310 --> 00:56:24.580
to prevent the secretion
of things like FSH and LH.

00:56:24.580 --> 00:56:27.820
Now, typically, people are not
getting pregnant every month.

00:56:27.820 --> 00:56:29.483
In fact, that's not possible.

00:56:29.483 --> 00:56:31.150
And part of the reason
it's not possible

00:56:31.150 --> 00:56:33.110
is that if the
fertilized egg implants,

00:56:33.110 --> 00:56:35.110
there are a number of
different hormone cascades

00:56:35.110 --> 00:56:39.340
that shut down the production
of things like GnRH, FSH, and LH

00:56:39.340 --> 00:56:42.730
in ways that prevent further
maturation of follicles

00:56:42.730 --> 00:56:43.930
and a follicular phase.

00:56:43.930 --> 00:56:47.240
But in the instance where
fertilization doesn't occur

00:56:47.240 --> 00:56:49.150
and menstruation occurs--

00:56:49.150 --> 00:56:52.120
and I should mention that
the duration of menstruation,

00:56:52.120 --> 00:56:54.500
the actual bleeding,
typically is anywhere from one

00:56:54.500 --> 00:56:55.840
to five days.

00:56:55.840 --> 00:56:57.580
The, quote, unquote,
"heaviness,"

00:56:57.580 --> 00:57:01.270
the lightness or heaviness of
that bleeding will depend on--

00:57:01.270 --> 00:57:04.870
you guessed it-- the amount of
progesterone that is secreted

00:57:04.870 --> 00:57:05.980
from the corpus luteum.

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:08.200
That's one of the
key players there.

00:57:08.200 --> 00:57:14.020
And if menstruation occurs,
well, then inhibin levels

00:57:14.020 --> 00:57:14.620
also drop.

00:57:14.620 --> 00:57:16.150
Progesterone levels also drop.

00:57:16.150 --> 00:57:20.140
And when that occurs,
there's a positive feedback

00:57:20.140 --> 00:57:23.240
signal up at the level
of the pituitary.

00:57:23.240 --> 00:57:25.300
The pituitary
literally can register

00:57:25.300 --> 00:57:28.030
how much inhibin and
progesterone and estrogen

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:29.570
is present in the bloodstream.

00:57:29.570 --> 00:57:32.800
And if those levels are
sufficiently low, well,

00:57:32.800 --> 00:57:36.850
then GnRH gets secreted again,
FSH gets secreted again,

00:57:36.850 --> 00:57:39.080
and LH gets secreted again.

00:57:39.080 --> 00:57:41.260
And the first half
the follicular phase

00:57:41.260 --> 00:57:44.480
of the menstrual cycle
initiates all over again.

00:57:44.480 --> 00:57:46.990
It's hard to overstate how
beautifully orchestrated

00:57:46.990 --> 00:57:48.760
this entire system is--

00:57:48.760 --> 00:57:51.400
The number of feedback loops
and feed-forward loops.

00:57:51.400 --> 00:57:53.380
I think if you can just
generally understand

00:57:53.380 --> 00:57:56.080
that the first half of the
menstrual/ovulatory cycle

00:57:56.080 --> 00:57:58.960
is marked by the maturation
of the follicles and FSH

00:57:58.960 --> 00:58:00.820
and that the second
half is marked

00:58:00.820 --> 00:58:03.190
by the accumulation
of progesterone

00:58:03.190 --> 00:58:04.870
and the thickening
of the uterine lining

00:58:04.870 --> 00:58:07.690
should fertilization
and implantation occur,

00:58:07.690 --> 00:58:09.820
I think that you will
certainly understand

00:58:09.820 --> 00:58:12.460
the female reproductive
cycle better than most people

00:58:12.460 --> 00:58:13.125
out there.

00:58:13.125 --> 00:58:15.250
It will also help you
understand a number of things

00:58:15.250 --> 00:58:17.720
that are sometimes associated
with the female reproductive

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:18.220
cycle.

00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:19.720
For instance, there
are data showing

00:58:19.720 --> 00:58:24.290
that, in many, not all, but
in many women, in the four

00:58:24.290 --> 00:58:27.010
to five days prior to
ovulation, there is

00:58:27.010 --> 00:58:28.810
a dramatic increase in libido.

00:58:28.810 --> 00:58:30.670
That dramatic
increase in libido is

00:58:30.670 --> 00:58:33.580
triggered by a number of
things, but some of those things

00:58:33.580 --> 00:58:36.100
include the spike
in FSH that occurs,

00:58:36.100 --> 00:58:40.510
the spike in LH that occurs,
and some associated increases

00:58:40.510 --> 00:58:44.030
in androgens, things like
DHEA and testosterone,

00:58:44.030 --> 00:58:47.740
which, just as in males
can be related to libido,

00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:49.240
in females trigger libido.

00:58:49.240 --> 00:58:51.940
You can imagine why this would
be an effective mechanism

00:58:51.940 --> 00:58:57.070
to have in place in females if
the goal, as it were, certainly

00:58:57.070 --> 00:59:00.800
of the egg, perhaps not
of the woman as a whole,

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:04.060
but if the goal is to
fertilize the egg--

00:59:04.060 --> 00:59:05.890
so increases in
libido just prior

00:59:05.890 --> 00:59:08.410
to the onset of ovulation.

00:59:08.410 --> 00:59:11.530
There's also been a lot
of discussion and interest

00:59:11.530 --> 00:59:14.920
and, frankly, data
exploring the malaise

00:59:14.920 --> 00:59:16.660
that it can occur
at certain portions

00:59:16.660 --> 00:59:18.820
of the menstrual cycle.

00:59:18.820 --> 00:59:21.130
And there's a lot of
misconception about this.

00:59:21.130 --> 00:59:25.750
A lot of people have focused
on the malaise that can occur

00:59:25.750 --> 00:59:27.730
around the time of bleeding.

00:59:27.730 --> 00:59:30.400
But there are
actually stronger data

00:59:30.400 --> 00:59:34.120
to support the fact that some,
again, some, not all women

00:59:34.120 --> 00:59:36.670
experience a kind of
malaise sometimes associated

00:59:36.670 --> 00:59:40.570
with anxiety, sometimes not,
that's associated with the mid

00:59:40.570 --> 00:59:44.400
to second half of
the luteal phase

00:59:44.400 --> 00:59:47.550
of the
ovulatory/menstrual cycle.

00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:51.150
And that, despite what
people commonly think,

00:59:51.150 --> 00:59:53.760
is not associated with
elevated levels of estrogen.

00:59:53.760 --> 00:59:56.520
It's actually associated with
the depletion in estrogen

00:59:56.520 --> 00:59:59.310
levels that can occur
during certain portions

00:59:59.310 --> 01:00:01.890
of that second half
of the luteal phase

01:00:01.890 --> 01:00:03.370
of the menstrual cycle.

01:00:03.370 --> 01:00:06.810
So again, this is
highly variable.

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:08.730
For some people, they
might not experience

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:11.490
any malaise at any point
during their menstrual cycle.

01:00:11.490 --> 01:00:13.060
Other individuals
also, for instance,

01:00:13.060 --> 01:00:15.440
might not experience any
variation in their libido

01:00:15.440 --> 01:00:17.190
at any point during
their menstrual cycle.

01:00:17.190 --> 01:00:18.840
Again, highly
variable, and yet there

01:00:18.840 --> 01:00:21.060
are some statistically
significant trends

01:00:21.060 --> 01:00:22.800
that have been
observed that tracked

01:00:22.800 --> 01:00:24.420
very specific
hormonal components

01:00:24.420 --> 01:00:25.740
within the menstrual cycle.

01:00:25.740 --> 01:00:28.620
Again, this will all
be very contextual.

01:00:28.620 --> 01:00:31.620
And of course, this can play out
in a number of different ways.

01:00:31.620 --> 01:00:33.870
So for instance, some women
experience very heightened

01:00:33.870 --> 01:00:35.580
levels of sensitivity
to caffeine

01:00:35.580 --> 01:00:37.650
at certain portions of
their menstrual cycle.

01:00:37.650 --> 01:00:40.942
Other women experience
more cramping than others

01:00:40.942 --> 01:00:42.900
at different portions of
their menstrual cycle.

01:00:42.900 --> 01:00:46.200
Tremendous variation from
individual to individual.

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:47.550
One of the--

01:00:47.550 --> 01:00:48.780
I view it as an advantage.

01:00:48.780 --> 01:00:53.310
But one of the things that
many females can really do

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:56.100
and experience because they have
cycles that occur every month

01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.800
that are fairly dramatic
in terms of their levels

01:00:58.800 --> 01:01:02.760
of hormones-- so for instance,
a more than 1,000-fold increase

01:01:02.760 --> 01:01:05.100
in progesterone during the
luteal phase of the menstrual

01:01:05.100 --> 01:01:07.110
cycle and, I should
also mention,

01:01:07.110 --> 01:01:10.350
a 200-fold increase in estrogen
during the period just prior

01:01:10.350 --> 01:01:11.160
to ovulation.

01:01:11.160 --> 01:01:14.393
That's why they always say
estrogen primes progesterone.

01:01:14.393 --> 01:01:16.560
That's what you learn in
kind of basic endocrinology

01:01:16.560 --> 01:01:17.860
when you're learning
the menstrual cycle.

01:01:17.860 --> 01:01:19.902
Estrogen in the first half
of the menstrual cycle

01:01:19.902 --> 01:01:21.900
primes progesterone
in the second half

01:01:21.900 --> 01:01:24.190
of the
ovulatory/menstrual cycle.

01:01:24.190 --> 01:01:27.930
Well, those estrogen increases
just prior to ovulation

01:01:27.930 --> 01:01:32.250
are in part responsible for
the increases in libido.

01:01:32.250 --> 01:01:35.490
But it's also the presence of
increased androgen just prior

01:01:35.490 --> 01:01:36.120
to ovulation.

01:01:36.120 --> 01:01:38.370
So there's a lot of
complex interplay.

01:01:38.370 --> 01:01:40.500
I think what we will
do is we will reserve

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:43.410
the discussion about
libido, per se,

01:01:43.410 --> 01:01:44.970
and some of the
other aspects related

01:01:44.970 --> 01:01:46.140
to sexual
differentiation that we

01:01:46.140 --> 01:01:48.140
were talking about earlier
for a future episode.

01:01:48.140 --> 01:01:49.740
But hopefully now
you have in mind

01:01:49.740 --> 01:01:51.990
what the ovulatory/menstrual
cycle is.

01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:55.420
It is a signal from the
brain, from the hypothalamus,

01:01:55.420 --> 01:01:57.660
which then triggers a
signal from the pituitary,

01:01:57.660 --> 01:02:02.040
an endocrine gland, which then
signals the release of hormones

01:02:02.040 --> 01:02:04.590
that travel to the
ovary and that control

01:02:04.590 --> 01:02:08.250
two things, maturation of eggs
and the identification of one

01:02:08.250 --> 01:02:13.650
egg in particular and then
preparation of the milieu,

01:02:13.650 --> 01:02:15.990
the environment in which
that fertilized egg could

01:02:15.990 --> 01:02:21.130
potentially land and mature
into a healthy embryo and child.

01:02:21.130 --> 01:02:23.140
So if you have that
framed up in your mind

01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:26.148
and even if you just
extracted maybe 10% to 15%

01:02:26.148 --> 01:02:27.690
of the hormones and
different aspects

01:02:27.690 --> 01:02:32.190
that I described up until now,
I would consider you far more

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:36.000
knowledgeable about this entire
process than 99% of people

01:02:36.000 --> 01:02:39.270
out there, certainly not
the OB/GYNs and urologists,

01:02:39.270 --> 01:02:42.490
but the 99% of
individuals out there.

01:02:42.490 --> 01:02:45.570
It also frames up for
us the second half

01:02:45.570 --> 01:02:47.980
of this whole story about
fertility and fertilization,

01:02:47.980 --> 01:02:52.740
which is the generation of sperm
and how the sperm eventually

01:02:52.740 --> 01:02:56.070
arrive at the egg and how
certain sperm are selected

01:02:56.070 --> 01:02:58.860
to potentially
fertilize that egg,

01:02:58.860 --> 01:03:01.150
whereas others never
really stand a chance.

01:03:01.150 --> 01:03:03.062
So next we're going
to talk about sperm.

01:03:03.062 --> 01:03:04.770
We're going to talk
about what sperm are,

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:06.978
where they are generated,
and how they are generated,

01:03:06.978 --> 01:03:09.390
and how they need to
travel both within the male

01:03:09.390 --> 01:03:12.840
and within the female in
order to allow fertilization

01:03:12.840 --> 01:03:13.920
to potentially occur.

01:03:13.920 --> 01:03:16.350
I'd like to take a
brief break and thank

